00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
this is a production of cornell  university  hello my name is larry smart i'm a  professor in the horticulture section of  the school of integrated plant science  and i'm based at cornell agritech in  geneva  i've been at cornell for 12 years now  previously i was a professor at the suny  college of environmental science and  forestry in syracuse  where i started studying shrub willow as  a bioenergy crop  so let's talk a little bit about  some of the urgent needs in society that  we can address with agriculture the most  urgent is to address climate change and  to reduce the amount of carbon and other  greenhouse gases in the atmosphere  shrub willow can address that issue  because it can be used as a source of  liquid biofuels  which to be honest are being replaced by  electric vehicles at this point  it can be used as a source of electric  power  by burning wood chips in power plants  which  again as we switch to electrification of  our automobiles  can  serve as a carbon neutral fuel source  for electrification  we can also use wood chips as a source  of heating fuel and we do that here at  cornell agritech  using willow that we grow on our farms  burned in a boiler that heats two of our  buildings just a mile away from where  the willow was grown  so local energy  we're also looking at willow for other  ecosystem services  willow can be planted in riparian buffer  zones to take up nutrients and protect  the water quality in our lakes and  watersheds  we're also looking at a dual purpose for  willow in that respect  in harvesting it or  growing it as a fodder crop  for small ruminants sheep and goats and  we see increasing production of sheep  and goats in new york right now  so those are the end uses  again right now  a push for biofuels in our breeding  program  inspired us to look at biomass  composition and we did a lot of work  to quantify the cellulose lignin and  hemicellulose in willow to try to  optimize it for conversion to biofuels  that era seems to be fading away  so really our main objective  in bioenergy crop breeding right now is  yield and sustainable yield but also  carbon sequestration  so can we also identify willows that  sequester carbon in their root systems  and making the entire system carbon  negative  coupled with that if we can look at high  nitrogen uptake  which will have the dual purpose of  enhancing  nutrient management and protecting our  water quality while also providing a  high quality  feed for sheep and goats  we get additional ecosystem services and  value for the farmer  so those are some of our goals in our  long-term breeding program  we work with many different species of  willow and many of them can hybridize  so  we have focused on topics of hybrid  vigor  many of our species have different  ploidy levels so we actually make  crosses between tetraploid and diploid  species and have done a lot of work to  characterize heterosis in triploid  species hybrids  and we do see consistent  heterosis  in our triploids which also have the  advantage of being sterile  which reduces their potential for  invasiveness  we have used multiple different trait  mapping approaches  to study willow genetics and to identify  markers  for marker assisted selection  although to date we have not been using  a lot of marker assisted selection in  our willow program we are very close to  identifying some of the major genes that  will be important for long-term genetic  improvement  through these different mapping  approaches  so we have used association mapping  or g-wasps  we have generated an f2 mapping  population of salex purpuria  and that is our reference species  we have worked with  the department of energy joint genome  institute to develop very high quality  pac-bio genome assemblies of a male and  female willow i should have mentioned  willow is dioecious  so making these crosses involves  crossing between a male and a female  sibling in a population  we  have also most recently developed  a series of f1 species hybrid mapping  populations  by crossing different diploid species  relatives to our reference species sale  of purpuria  and we believe that will be very  fruitful in the long run  for introducing unique alleles from  these other diverse species  we're particularly interested in  resistance to  rust rust is our major pathogen threat  to willow  and some of these related species to  salix purpuria  bring resistance to our common rust  species here in the united states  so this is the latest  trial that we have planted this past  spring  for genetic mapping it includes about  425  individuals in our s2 mapping population  derived from our monitors individual as  well as another about 180 diverse  unrelated individuals  for  g wasp mapping or association mapping we  plant in three plant plots in a complete  randomized block design  in single rows  so it's relatively easy to cut back  with our tractor mounted single row  harvester and again the goal would be to  identify genes for rust resistance  for resistance to  insect pests  and  to identify variation in other  morphological traits which will lead to  yield increases  so you're probably not accustomed to  growing willow as an agricultural crop  what you see behind me are plants  that  are in their second growing season after  cutback  so this is a short rotation copper  system where we originally plant from  cuttings so our breeding program is  aimed at producing clonal cultivars  propagated through stem cuttings which  we produce in nursery beds  those cuttings can be planted  mechanically  with a planter that will take long whips  and cut on the fly into  eight inch cuttings which it plants in  the ground  we typically will plant in a double row  spacing 30 inches between those rows  two feet between plants which has been  optimized for harvesting with a  self-propelled forage harvester with  large saw blades on the front  so  after original establishment by cuttings  we typically will cut back the field or  composite that stimulates rapid regrowth  of multiple small diameter stems in year  two  and then we will typically allow those  plants to grow three or four years  before we come in with the forage  harvester  to cut them back down to the ground uh  as low as we can get producing wood  chips  that again we deliver to a power plant  uh or to a conversion facility  the plants will vigorously  re-sprout the following spring after  that harvest and we can  continue and sustain that cycle of three  to four year harvest cycles for at least  seven to ten  times meaning that the crop is sustained  and continues to sequester carbon  for 25 to 30 years  so these willow crops  need to  have durable disease resistance for more  than 25 or 30 years  making that one of our most urgent  breeding needs is to identify primarily  rust resistance but also resistance to  insect pests  which will become  more persistent with more life cycles  as  temperatures begin to increase  through climate change  finally we have been looking at sex  determination and the mechanism of the  sex chromosome in salix especially as it  relates to poplar and the evolution of  sex determination in the cellar casey  very interestingly there are different  mechanisms for sex determination across  the kc  either x y  or z w z w being  where the female is the heterogametic  sex  as it is in birds and reptiles  so we have flip-flopping of the sex  determination system across the sala  casey and also movement of the sex  determination region from different  chromosomes in the species that we work  with the sex determination is on  chromosome 15  but in other species of celix it has  moved to chromosome 7  and in poplar it can be found on  chromosome 19.  so those are interesting topics of uh  what is the evolutionary driver for  daishi in the saleh casey and what is  the driver for  zw versus xy  and the movement of the sex  determination region  uh across different chromosomes  is the sex determination region linked  to  genes that are driving  sex dimorphism for example so we've been  doing a lot of research looking at sex  dimorphic traits most recently  the final tool that we have to address  that question is we do find monicious  varieties of willow  so individuals that produce both male  and female flowers on a single  individual  we have used that to create an s1 and  now an s2 mapping population  which we have genotyped and have  recently planted  here for phenotyping so that will give  us yet another  mapping tool to identify important  traits in salex purpuria  in the s2 and we also expect to be able  to identify loci that are responsible  for inbreeding depression in that  population  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

